      "topic": "security",
      "confidence": "high",
      "title": "canManage() grants manage-all-groups on the VIEW scope",
      "body": "`canManage()` calls `hasPermission(null, VIEW, MANAGE)`, so a permission on all groups that only has the `view` scope makes it return true. `GroupsResource.java:181` calls `auth.groups().requireManage()` before creating a top-level group. An admin with view-only access to all groups can therefore create groups. The `testViewGroups` test only grants VIEW and never checks that create is still forbidden, so it would not catch this. Use only `AdminPermissionsSchema.MANAGE` here. The matching javadoc in GroupPermissionEvaluator (lines 75-76) says VIEW or MANAGE and should be corrected too.",
      "anchor": "diff"
    },
    {
      "path": "tests/base/src/test/java/org/keycloak/tests/admin/authz/fgap/GroupResourceTypeEvaluationTest.java",
      "line": 250,
      "severity": "consider",
      "topic": "tests",
      "confidence": "medium",
      "title": "No negative test for view-only permission",
      "body": "`testViewGroups` grants only VIEW but never asserts that creating a group or updating one is still forbidden. A check like that would catch the VIEW-grants-manage problem in `GroupPermissionsV2.canManage()`.",
      "anchor": "diff"
    }
  ],
  "contracts": [],
  "summary": "I found one real defect. In V2, `GroupPermissionsV2.canManage()` accepts the `view` scope, so an admin with view-only access to all groups can create groups. I also suggest a negative test for it. The `UsersResource` and `BruteForceUsersResource` refactors still filter users with `canView` in `toRepresentation`, so I found no regression there. I only read the code and did not build or run anything.",
